UP|52|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|It did work out because North has at least 7 tricks if diamonds behave and it turns out 1N is a lot easier to play than 3@D.|
PG| ***** 71 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^^B@2^B@2^*BA Kib: ^*NWhy is 3@D weak here?|
PG| ***** 72 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Because a jump raise in competition is weak. If you want to make a limit raise you cue bid 2@S. By the way, the singleton @H10 is another reason why 1N is a bit problematical.|
PG| ***** 73 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|On the other hand, particularly since hearts were not bid, it is likely that I have some heart cards and some heart length. In 3@D we take 5 diamonds, 2 spades, 1 heart, and a club ruff making 3@D also.
|
PG| ***** 74 ***** |


